Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: retire an agent silent past the topology ageing, and rejoin it when heard again

The controller never retired an agent that went silent: an unpowered extender's device,
radios, BSSs and stations stayed in DeviceList, RadioList, BSSList, STAList and the DataElements
indefinitely, and an optimizer or a steer could still target its BSSs. 0047's liveness query only
suppressed a Wi-Fi agent from the internal topology (its identity, rows and DataElements kept),
and a wired agent sends no neighbor-only notification, so its loss was not even probed.

Track every agent by the last CMDU received from it (0047's note_agent_activity, on every frame
the controller hands to an em of that agent, wired or Wi-Fi alike). IEEE 1905.1-2013 8.2.1.1 has
a 1905.1 management entity send a Topology Discovery message at least every 60 s; the spec sets
that period, not when a silent device is gone. This controller's margin is three of those
periods, 180 s, in which it has queried the agent twelve times (every 15 s) with no CMDU back
(the Broadband Forum's 1905.1 reference stack ages a node out after 90 s, its GC_MAX_AGE). The
retirement runs on the manager thread, before the dirty models are written, in two passes so no
command, em or row meets a half-removed agent:
- first every radio is queued as stale and retired the way 0253 retires a radio an agent stopped
  reporting: out of every command, its em stopped and out of the map, its radio and operating
  class rows deleted, its BSSs removed from the model and their rows deleted at the BSS list
  update;
- once the model has no radio and nothing left to write, its stations' rows and its device's row
  are deleted, its node is taken out of the network topology and the model is deleted from the
  list (deinitialised, as delete_data_model does), the last two under the topology lock. The
  topology holds the model's address and every topology update walks all its nodes: a node left
  in is read after the model is freed (the lab, 20 Oct: the controller crashed at the first
  update after a retirement). The DataElements are built from the models, so the agent leaves
  them too. No pointer into a model is kept across a retirement or a model made again: the
  co-located agent's AL MAC and the controller's interface MAC are copied first, the controller's
  from its own model. The network the model list hands out is the one held in its first model,
  and that can be the retired agent's model (the lab, 21 Oct: the controller crashed at the next
  model's check after retiring an agent whose model had been made again at its rejoin and come
  first).
The co-located agent and the controller's own model are never retired. For 600 s after the
controller's start nothing is retired, and an agent known only from the database (not yet heard
in this run) starts its clock at the first pass after that, so agents that reboot with the
gateway get time to come back (operator pods on the bench returned 274-392 s after the
gateway's boot). A session that merely restarts (an EMOSA restart, a backhaul move) is silent for
seconds, well inside the margin. A stall on the controller's own side is not agents gone: on a
pass where the co-located agent, or every agent, has been unheard for 60 s nothing is retired, nor
for 60 s after the stall ends, so the frames queued in it are handled and the queries answered
first (the bench, 20 Oct: a 58 s pause in every agent's answers at once).

The retired agent's identity is kept (its AL MAC, profile, EasyMesh+ flag and its radios' bands).
Any 1905 frame from that AL MAC, retiring or retired, a Topology Discovery too (it maps to no em),
starts its rejoin, so an agent that only lost its link for a while (it never reboots, so it never
searches again) comes back: its model is made again if it was removed, as the AP-Autoconfiguration
Search makes it (an M1 needs a model to be taken), then it is sent a targeted
AP-Autoconfiguration Renew per band of its radios (the controller's own renew, addressed to its
AL MAC from the controller's AL node), at most three, 60, 120 and 240 s apart, until its M1s have
rebuilt a radio, logged as its rejoin. With no M1 after the third (an agent whose own side is
down still multicasts its Topology Discovery, e.g. an EMOSA agent whose pod is away) it is retired
again and its discoveries alone are ignored for 1800 s; any other frame from it still starts a
rejoin at once, and an agent that boots again searches and onboards as any agent does.

Upstream-Status: Pending
